1. Create the EFS filesystem in AWS

2. Give the EFS CSI driver the required AWS credentials using a Kubernetes Secret.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 2.1 Create an AWS IAM user for the EFS CSI driver
  2.2 assign this permission "AmazonElasticFileSystemFullAccess" to the user
  2.3 Create an access key (IAM → Users → k3s-efs-csi → Security credentials)
  2.4 Create the Kubernetes Secret
      kubectl create secret generic aws-efs-credentials -n kube-system \
    --from-literal=key_id='AKIA1234567xxxx' \
    --from-literal=access_key='xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx'

3) Test EFS connectivity BEFORE Kubernetes integration
-------------------------------------------------------
   3.1  create a director 
        mkdir kubefile 
   3.2. mount it now with efs url 
   sudo tee -a /etc/fstab > /dev/null <<EOF
fs-056cced4522b8b845.efs.us-east-2.amazonaws.com:/ /home/ubuntu/Kubefile nfs4 nfsvers=4.1,rsize=1048576,wsize=1048576,hard,timeo=600,retrans=2,noresvport,_netdev 0 0
EOF
   3.3 mount complete
   ------------------
    sudo mount -a
   3.4. Confirm it 
   ---------------
        df -h


4) Use HELM to Install the EFS CSI driver to kubernetes cluster
---------------------------------------------------------------
   4.1 open powershell as an admin and run choco install kubernetes-helm
   4.2 open gitbash and export your kubeconfig file to environmental variable. 
       export KUBECONFIG="/c/Users/smart/Documents/Cluster_keys/New_singleCluster.yaml"
       or use poershell 
       $env:KUBECONFIG="C:\Users\smart\Documents\Cluster_keys\New_singleCluster.yaml"

    4.3 Add EFS-CSI driver.
    ------------------------
        helm repo add aws-efs-csi-driver https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/aws-efs-csi-driver/
        
        helm repo update
    4.4 Create a values.yaml file and add this content in it
    --------------------------------------------------------
    notepad values.yaml 
    " controller:
  env:
    - name: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: aws-efs-credentials
          key: key_id

    - name: AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: aws-efs-credentials
          key: access_key

storageClasses:
  - name: efs-dynamic
    parameters:
      provisioningMode: efs-ap
      fileSystemId: fs-056cced4522b8b845
      directoryPerms: "700"
      gidRangeStart: "1000"
      gidRangeEnd: "2000"
      basePath: "/dynamic_provisioning"
      ensureUniqueDirectory: "true"
    reclaimPolicy: Delete
    volumeBindingMode: Immediate"


    4.4. Install the EFS- Driver plugins and StorageClass
    ------------------------------------------------------
    helm upgrade --install aws-efs-csi-driver aws-efs-csi-driver/aws-efs-csi-driver --namespace kube-system -f values.yaml
    
   3.5 verify it now .
   --------------------
   kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep efs

   4.6 Verify Storage Class 
  --------------------------
  kubectl get storageclass


5.0 Test it now with Pod deployment
-----------------------------------
5.1 create a file pod-with-pvc.yaml

"apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: efs-dynamic-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteMany
  storageClassName: efs-dynamic
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx-efs
spec:
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:latest
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80
      volumeMounts:
        - name: efs-storage
          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html

  volumes:
    - name: efs-storage
      persistentVolumeClaim:
        claimName: efs-dynamic-pvc" 

5.2 Apply it 
-------------
kubectl apply -f pod-with-pvc.yaml

5.6 verify it by 
-----------------
kubectl get pod 
kubectl get pvc
kubectl get sc

  

